orms as movement skill; this unit teaches performance quality and rehearsal, and names that unit in its connections line. Showing: the partner and group phrase for another class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a showing day, about day 17 of the unit): DA:Pr4.1.Ib, a look-for: accents and syncopation on the counts, the tempo change and the music cue taken; DA:Pr4.1.Ic, a look-for: energy shown through the whole body with two contrasting dynamics. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then a kept-piece day): DA:Pr4.1.Ia, a look-for: a low balance at a contrasting level held with the partner or the group (or its solo version); DA:Pr5.1.Ia, a look-for: the 32-count phrase performed from memory with its spacing and timing; DA:Pr5.1.Ib, a kept piece: the healthy-habits plan for the invented dancer and how it supports the dancer's goals, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Pr5.1.Ic, a kept piece: the group's rehearsal plan, the observer's notes and a written justification of two practice choices. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: purposeful space and aerial work (Level 2), lifts (never). Tools: step cards, the balance card with its solo version, a rehearsal plan sheet, an observer's sheet, a beat keeper or its printed count card, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps or a drum for the beat. Short path: 18 days | First owning unit because every later Level 1 unit dances phrases that need clear elements and a rehearsal routine; the rehearsal-plan model starts here, and unit 5's concert uses it. Longest with unit 4: six ids, three of them performed and scored live | Candidate: a third work with a strong rhythm from Asia, described and linked | Hazards and controls: balances low to the floor, by agreement, with a solo version always offered; no lifts; partner contact by agreement and never required; tap is watched, not done in hard shoes on the class floor (taps damage floors and ankles without training); the warm-up before every dancing day