tem: a repeated pattern and the purpose it serves; DA:Re7.1.Ib, a written item: how an element carries intent in each dance's stated cultural setting. End-of-unit assessment: DA:Re8.1.Ia, a kept piece: a comparison of two studied dances, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Re9.1.Ia, a written item in the end sitting: the unseen dance's expression analyzed with three criteria and dance terms; DA:Cr3.1.Ia, a look-for on the showing and the log: two revisions to devices or structure and an evaluation of each; DA:Cr3.1.Ib, a kept piece: one section recorded in both methods with two differences named. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: weighing societal values (Level 2), a whole dance notated (Level 3). Tools: work cards, the notation sheets for both methods, the criteria card, the change log, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil and printed sheets. Short path: 19 days | After unit 3 because the revision reworks unit 3's dance and the analysis uses its structure words; before unit 5 because the concert shows the revised dance and research asks for the analysis habits | Candidate: a dance from Asia for the comparison, described and linked | Niicugni: the sensitivity agent reads Emily Johnson's own published words first; the unit names her as a Yup'ik maker in her own words, the dance is watched only, and no student is asked about their own heritage. Maqoma's solo tells of a chief who fought colonial rule: the agent reads the artist's own published words first; the unit tells it plainly from them, with no role play. Hazards and controls: as unit 2. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
