Each written sitting: set-up 2 minutes, the unseen phrase danced twice by the teacher or the description read
(about 4), written items alone (about 33), collecting 3, inside the 45 minutes. Every student's evidence is collected
once in a window; no piece is scored twice. Days above are course days of the level (Level 2 starts on band day 161,
Level 3 on day 321, Level 4 on day 481). Weeks: 17 and 32 in Levels 1 to 3, 15 and 28 in Level 4, the year-end
checks of Levels 1 to 3 in the finals week (CALENDAR.md rule 4).

## Cross-subject

One dance row of the band carries a cross_subject lean: da-g9-technique (unit 2, Level 1) names physical education's
g9-dance-design and health's g9-phw-evaluate-habits. A Level 1 student may be in any grade from 9 to 12, so each
lean is same-year only for a student in grade 9; the unit teaches what it needs itself either way, and every lean
names a course a school must offer (physical education's two required years, health's semester; lesson 32).

### Physical education, grade 9 (same year for a grade 9 student; name each other)

| Used here | Contact | If physical education has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Unit 2 (Level 1 course days 13 to 50, weeks 3 to 10): performance quality and rehearsal of partner and group phrases (da-g9-technique) | physical-education:g9-dance-design: physical education grades 9 to 12, grade 9 unit 5, "Dance by design" (days 53 to 64 of its grade 9 year, weeks 22 to 26): a set sequence learned and a short phrase designed, as movement skill | The dance unit teaches the counts and the phrase it dances itself from the bridge; it teaches no motor cue. Boundary: physical education teaches the sequence and dance forms as movement skill, this course teaches choreography, performing for an audience and response; each unit names the other in its connections line, so nothing is taught twice in a year. |

### Health, grades 9 to 12 (the health semester)

| Used here | Contact | If health has not taught it yet |
|---|---|---|
| Unit 2: a healthy-habits plan for an invented dancer (DA:Pr5.1.Ib, da-g9-technique) | health:g9-phw-evaluate-habits: health grades 9 to 12, unit 5, "Habits that prevent" (days 16 to 18 of the semester): habits that prevent illness and injury, judged with their sources | The dance unit teaches the dance habits it needs itself (warm-up, cool-down, rest, water, sleep, safe landing) through the invented dancer; it does not teach health's evaluation of evidence. A semester course may fall in either half or another grade, so no unit waits on it. |

### English language arts, grades 9 to 12

Written analysis, comparison and critique (units 4, 9, 14 and 17) practice argument writing with evidence
(W.9-10.1, W.11-12.1): practiced, not owned. If ELA has not yet taught the claim-and-evidence form that year, the
dance unit's analysis frame gives it (a claim, a time-stamped or described moment as evidence, a reason) and the
teacher models it on the first analysis day.

### Social studies

Units 4, 5, 9, 14 and 15 teach their works with time and place using social studies' sourcing habits (STANDARD.md 6;
who made it, when, where, for what): practiced, not owned. Each unit teaches the setting it uses on the work card
itself (bar criterion 7). No math beyond counts of eight and beats is used (lesson 13).

