5: ## The course in brief
6: 
7: - **Units.** 19: five in each of Levels 1 to 3, four in Level 4. Levels 1 to 3 share one shape, so a thread comes
8:   back one level harder in the same slot a year later: an opening unit (the Level 1 bridge, or the Level 2 and 3
9:   review that doubles as placement; no owned id), a body, rhythm and rehearsal unit (elements and technique), a
10:   making unit (ideas and form), a watching and changing unit (revision and response) and a production and research
11:   unit (staging and connecting) that ends the level with the class concert. Each owning unit joins two progression
12:   threads, one of creating or performing and one of performing, responding or connecting, so every unit makes or
13:   dances, studies another artist's dance and shows (bar criteria 4 and 5). Level 4 is one sustained project in four
14:   phases (a series of dances from a proposal, critique, a led showing, a capstone), with its checkpoints as check
15:   days and critique on its respond days (APPROACH "High school levels and music").
16: - **Days.** 640: 160 a level for four levels (480 for the three levels a school must offer to reach Advanced; one
17:   level is a complete course, STANDARD.md 3.2). Each level: 156 days in its units, 2 course-level check days (one
18:   mid-year, one year-end) and 2 respond days, one after each check. One lesson of about 45 minutes a day, five a
19:   week, 32 weeks: 160 lessons of 45 minutes is 120 hours, the time the national Proficient descriptor names for one
20:   high school arts course (STANDARD.md 3.2 and 3.4). The 45 minutes hold the set-up (chairs and desks back, the floor
21:   checked, spots marked: about 3 minutes; students dance in their own comfortable clothes, and no change of clothes
22:   is assumed), a warm-up (about 7), the teaching, dancing or writing, a cool-down with the closing line or log entry
23:   (about 4) and putting the room back (about 3); a readiness or retrieval task takes ten minutes. Each unit's days
24:   include its end-of-unit assessment and, since every owning unit is over 25 days, its mid-unit assessment. Unit
25:   counts follow the rows' load: the body and rehearsal unit and the watching and changing unit each own six ids (in
26:   the first, three performed and three planned or analyzed; in the second, four written response ids beside a
27:   revision and a notation task), so they are longest (38 days in Level 1, 40 in Levels 2 and 3); the making unit
28:   owns four ids (34 days); the production unit owns five ids and holds the concert's rehearsals (34 days in Level 1,
29:   36 in Levels 2 and 3, where a whole concert and then concerts in more than one place are staged). The Level 1
30:   bridge is 12 days, the Level 2 and 3 reviews 6. In Level 4 the studio phase is longest (70 days: proposal, a series
31:   of dances and its revisions), then the led showing (40), the critique set (24) and the capstone (22). These are
32:   course days: the school calendar (docs/courses/CALENDAR.md) places one a school day, so course day n of a level
33:   falls in that level's week n / 5, rounded up. National time figure: STANDARD.md 3.4 carries the dance educators'
34:   association's K to 8 figures only; its high school lines are not read at source, so this frame states none (For
35:   the lead 2). The course gives 225 minutes a week, 120 hours a level, and 60 hours on the short path. The course is
36:   written so a teacher without dance training can teach it; a school with a dance specialist uses that teacher.
37: - **The short path.** A school that cannot give dance every day (a semester, a rotation or alternate days) keeps every
38:   unit and every id of Levels 1 to 3 in 80 days by merging the days each unit marks (STANDARD.md 3.2). Days on that
39:   path: Level 1, units of 6, 18, 16, 19 and 17 days; Levels 2 and 3, units of 3, 20, 17, 20 and 16 days; each level's
40:   two checks keep 1 check day and 1 respond day (76 in units and 4 for checks: 80). The bridge's 6 days are 7.5
41:   percent of 80, inside the bar's 10 percent limit on days outside the course's standards (STANDARD.md 3.2). Fluency
42:   days fold into the warm-ups, and each row's understanding days merge into one; a merged day never drops a
43:   critique, a revision, a rehearsal before a showing, a mid-unit or end-of-unit assessment or a respond day. Level 4
44:   has no short path: STANDARD.md 3.2 sets the 80-day path for the three levels of each discipline only. The scope and
45:   sequence marks which days merge.
46: - **Standard.** National Core Arts Standards, 2014, dance performance standards, by id only, each named with its line
47:   in data/standards/ncas-performance-standards-words.txt, never quoted (STANDARD.md 2). The level rule binds
48:   (STANDARD.md 3.2): Level 1 claims only the 21 Proficient ids (`.Ia`, `.Ib`, `.Ic`), Level 2 only the 21 Accomplished
49:   ids (`.II`), Level 3 only the 21 Advanced ids (`.III`): 63 in all, the count in STANDARD.md 3.2, every one taught and
50:   assessed in its own level. Level 4 owns no id: it assesses the Level 3 `.IIIa` ids again on new, self-directed work
51:   (the rows' `~` codes, owned in Level 3). Advanced is claimed by assessed work, not by finishing Level 3: a student who
52:   ends Level 3 at "partly" on an id has Level 4 to reach it. No grade 8 id is claimed (the bridge teaches grade 8
53:   techniques and words as readiness only). Every level touches all four artistic processes (creating, performing,
54:   responding, connecting; bar criterion 1). In the Units table the C3 column is headed "Processes, anchors and works":
55:   the processes and anchor standards the unit works on, and the dances it studies. Each id's action was checked
56:   against its words-file line and the rows; the frame could not open the national text itself (no downloads in this
57:   job), so each unit plan checks its ids' actions against the national standard's own text before it is written, as
58:   the pilot's fix pass found a words line can drift (For the lead 3 names the two ids where the course's rules shape
59:   how an id is met). Check set: each unit plan checks itself against the words-file lines of its ids, the nine
60:   criteria of the arts part of docs/ADOPTION-BAR-SUBJECTS.md and the lens's error list (docs/agents/lenses/arts.md);
61:   the "Check set adds" column names what that is expected to add. Values rule (DESK-ANSWERS 2026-11-12) holds.
62:   scripts/standards.py checks every id against the id file; each level's scope and sequence still states its id count
63:   (21) against STANDARD.md 3.2.
64: - **Entry and placement.** A student new to dance starts at Level 1, whatever their grade; Level 1's first unit
65:   assumes no earlier arts learning. Each level requires the one before or a placement: the Level 2 and 3 review units
66:   double as placement, where a student new to the school shows work scored against the previous level's year-end
67:   check and its last unit's guide, and starts at the next level if it meets them (STANDARD.md 3.2). The scope and
68:   sequence names the level, never a grade.
69: - **More than one level in one room.** Many schools teach dance levels together. Because Levels 1 to 3 share the
70:   same five slots, a mixed room runs the same slot at once (body and rehearsal, making, watching and changing,
71:   production), each student on their own level's rows, ids, guide and checks; the warm-up, the works watched and the
72:   concert are shared. The scope and sequence says this once, on its first page.
73: - **Dance here, motor skill in physical education.** Dance in this course is the art: making, performing for an
74:   audience, responding to and connecting dance. Movement sequences and dance forms as movement skill are physical
75:   education's (STANDARD.md 6; physical education grade 9 unit 5, "Dance by design"). This course teaches no motor cue
76:   as such: it teaches choreography, performance quality, rehearsal and response, and names the physical education
77:   unit in unit 2's connections line (Cross-subject, below).
78: - **Base text.** None. Dance is Curio Class's own (docs/sources/BASE-TEXTS.md; the open-material scout,
79:   docs/reviews/arts-open-material-2026-11-16.md, found few open dance recordings). Dances studied are shown from
80:   public domain films (early films in the Library of Congress's collections) and, for living or recent makers and
81:   for every tradition, from a description in our own words with a link to the maker's, the company's or an archive's
82:   own page, never a copy or an embed (APPROACH "Rights"). Each dance named below is a pick the unit plan keeps unless
83:   its rights line, checked on the item's own page, fails; the unit plan confirms each title, year and maker on that
84:   page and records whether a maker is living, as of its date. A swapped dance must keep the unit's ids met and the
85:   band's range. The band's dances come from Africa (South Africa, Senegal through the A%cole des Sables, Sierra
86:   Leone through Asadata Dafora), the Americas (the United States including the Yup'ik people through Emily Johnson,
87:   Brazil), Asia (Taiwan, Korea, Japan, and Bangladesh and India through Akram Khan's story), Europe (Denmark,
88:   Belgium, the Netherlands, Germany, the United Kingdom, France and Russia through The Rite of Spring) and the
89:   Pacific (Samoa and New Zealand), from 1836 to today, with living makers in every level (APPROACH "Works and their
90:   settings"). None repeats a dance named in the Dance K to 2, 3 to 5 or 6 to 8 frames as they stood on 2026-11-25;
91:   a few makers come back with a different work (For the lead 9). The unseen dance in every check is a short Curio Class original
92:   danced live by the teacher from a step card, or a written description, never an embed (APPROACH "Scoring creative
93:   work"). Curio Class writes every step card, work card, prompt card, notation sheet, record sheet, process log page,
94:   family note and every unseen dance.
95: - **Techniques that get harder (bar criterion 2).** Body and rhythm: low partner balances at contrasting levels,
96:   syncopation and accents, energy through the whole body (Level 1); purposeful space in ground and aerial sequences,
97:   unpredictable time from an inner rhythm, phrases started with energy and linked (Level 2); the fullest range of
98:   space toward an audience, layered rhythms, dynamics across a broad range (Level 3). Making: many sources tested for
99:   one piece and an artist's statement (Level 1), invented and established movement blended across styles and a
100:   statement of aesthetic (Level 2), risk, a personal voice and a statement of cultural and artistic viewpoint (Level
101:   3), a self-directed series from a proposal (Level 4). Revising and recording: devices and structure reworked and
102:   two notation methods compared on one section (Level 1), revision against criteria and a plan to record a whole
103:   dance (Level 2), every revision logged and defended and a complete dance notated (Level 3). Rehearsing and staging:
104:   a rehearsal planned with peers and analyzed, a production design option chosen (Level 1); solo and group
105:   rehearsals planned, a whole concert staged from a production plan (Level 2); rehearsals led, concerts staged in
106:   more than one place (Level 3); the student's own showing led (Level 4).
107: - **Day kinds.** As docs/GLOSSARY.md ("teaching day") and APPROACH "Kinds of day". Understanding: a phrase, an element
108:   or a device shown in named steps with the step card, or a dance watched with the unit's words (the artistic process
109:   "responding" is taught here, never on a respond day). Fluency: short repeated practice with feedback (the warm-up,
110:   a phrase run three times for recall, a syncopation drill, a notation drill of eight counts); in high school it is
111:   the seven-minute warm-up and two or three fluency days a unit. Application: making, rehearsing, drafting a
112:   statement or analysis, and revising; the second application day holds the unit's one critique (describe, ask,
113:   suggest), and the next day's revision is application too. Check: a showing scored live on the guide, or a written
114:   sitting with unseen items. Respond: a second try at the part marked "not yet", or a re-rehearsal before a second
115:   showing. A unit runs roughly: understanding, fluency, application, the mid-unit assessment and its respond day,
116:   then understanding, fluency, application with the critique, revision, the end-of-unit assessment, respond.
117: - **Scored evidence.** Every owned id has an end-of-unit or mid-unit item or a look-for, named id by id in the unit's
118:   "Evidence, id by id" line. A progression row's "Assessed by" note names one form for the row, not one item for each
119:   id, so it is never the whole line. Items come in four kinds: a look-for on a showing's guide (three to five
120:   look-fors, met, partly or not yet), marked live as each group dances; a written item in a sitting on an unseen
121:   Curio Class dance or description; a kept piece (a process log entry, an artist's statement, a notation sheet, a
122:   rehearsal or production plan) drafted, given written feedback and revised on earlier counted days, then scored;
123:   and a spoken item in a peer discussion, marked live. Two kinds of check day, never mixed on one day. A showing day:
124:   set-up 3, warm-up 7, six groups of five dance for about 4 minutes each (24) while the rest are the audience,

