324: | 1 | Level 1 | The dancer's toolkit (bridge). *What do dancers need to know and do before they can make and perform a dance together?* | 12 |  | None | Performing (Pr4, Pr5) and responding (Re7) touched as readiness; creating (Cr1 to Cr3) as readiness. Works: Annabelle Whitford, Annabelle Serpentine Dance (United States, 1895, a public domain film in the Library of Congress's collections), shown: energy, space and time in one short solo; the class's own phrases | The Level 1 bridge (STANDARD.md 3.2): it teaches the grade 8 techniques and words Level 1 needs, as readiness, and claims no grade 8 id and no Level 1 id; it assumes no earlier dance. Taught: the class warm-up and cool-down and why each step is there; the dance elements (body, energy, space, time) with energy words; accent and timing in counts of eight; phrase recall (a Curio Class phrase learned, run, recalled the next day); rehearsal goals; start, middle and finish and four choreographic tools (repetition, canon, retrograde, change of level); the inspiration list and a movement challenge from one source; style words; the critique routine (describe, ask, suggest) with try-a-change; the process log begun as the change log; eight counts written in motif symbols; stage terms, the performing space and etiquette; look and say, describe and interpret, the reason-because frame and a criteria card; the like-me line, two ties for a dance and research notes. Words: element, energy, accent, phrase, canon, motif. Showing: a 16-count class phrase with one tool added, for the class. Evidence: none scored for a standard; the readiness record (the phrase recalled, an element named in the film, eight counts in motif symbols) sorts students met, partly or not yet, and the results shape the first days of units 2 and 3. Out: every Level 1 id (units 2 to 5). Tools: step cards, the element card, the motif symbol sheet, the process log, Curio Class's own. Free path: floor tape or chalk, the voice for counts. Short path: 6 days | First because every Level 1 unit needs the warm-up, the elements, the log and the critique routine, and Level 1 assumes no earlier arts learning; 12 days, the most STANDARD.md 3.2 allows, because a class may hold students with three years of middle school dance beside students with none | Candidate: a second short public domain film from outside the United States for the elements look | Hazards and controls: the floor checked and cleared; bare feet or soft-soled shoes, no socks on a smooth floor; the warm-up before any dancing; no lifts, no inverted moves; partner contact only by agreement. Some students have never danced before others: no one dances alone for the class. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
325: | 2 | Level 1 | Body, rhythm and rehearsal. *How do dancers make a phrase clear, alive and repeatable, and how do they practice to get there?* | 38 | da-g9-elements; da-g9-technique | DA:Pr4.1.Ia; DA:Pr4.1.Ib; DA:Pr4.1.Ic; DA:Pr5.1.Ia; DA:Pr5.1.Ib; DA:Pr5.1.Ic | Performing (Pr4, Pr5); responding through the works watched (review); connecting through the physical education pair named below. Works: Michelle Dorrance and Dorrance Dance, SOUNDspace (United States, 2013; tap, the dancers' feet as the music; the company's own page), described and linked: syncopation and accents; Vincent Mantsoe, Gula Matari (South Africa, 1993, a solo; the unit plan confirms title and year and records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the artist's or a presenting theater's page: energy carried through the whole body | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's elements, accent and timing, warm-up and phrase recall. DA:Pr4.1.Ia: build partner and group sections with low balances (counterbalance, shared weight at floor level) at contrasting levels; the id allows lifts or balances, and balances meet it (no lifts, APPROACH "Safe use"), with a solo version of every balance. DA:Pr4.1.Ib: use syncopation and accents at varied tempos, take cues from the accompaniment, and link breath to phrasing. DA:Pr4.1.Ic: carry energy and dynamics through the whole body so phrases show variety. DA:Pr5.1.Ia: use technical skill to retain and perform a 32-count Curio Class phrase. DA:Pr5.1.Ib: plan healthy habits for dance and daily life (warm-up, cool-down, rest, water, sleep) for an invented dancer, with food through that invented dancer, and say how the plan supports the dancer's goals. DA:Pr5.1.Ic: plan a rehearsal with peers, analyze a run of the phrase with an observer's sheet or the school's recording, and justify the practice choices. Words: syncopation, accent, balance, contrast, retain, rehearsal plan. Physical education boundary: grade 9 unit 5 teaches set sequences and dance forms as movement skill; this unit teaches performance quality and rehearsal, and names that unit in its connections line. Showing: the partner and group phrase for another class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a showing day, about day 17 of the unit): DA:Pr4.1.Ib, a look-for: accents and syncopation on the counts, the tempo change and the music cue taken; DA:Pr4.1.Ic, a look-for: energy shown through the whole body with two contrasting dynamics. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then a kept-piece day): DA:Pr4.1.Ia, a look-for: a low balance at a contrasting level held with the partner or the group (or its solo version); DA:Pr5.1.Ia, a look-for: the 32-count phrase performed from memory with its spacing and timing; DA:Pr5.1.Ib, a kept piece: the healthy-habits plan for the invented dancer and how it supports the dancer's goals, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Pr5.1.Ic, a kept piece: the group's rehearsal plan, the observer's notes and a written justification of two practice choices. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: purposeful space and aerial work (Level 2), lifts (never). Tools: step cards, the balance card with its solo version, a rehearsal plan sheet, an observer's sheet, a beat keeper or its printed count card, Curio Class's own. Free path: claps or a drum for the beat. Short path: 18 days | First owning unit because every later Level 1 unit dances phrases that need clear elements and a rehearsal routine; the rehearsal-plan model starts here, and unit 5's concert uses it. Longest with unit 4: six ids, three of them performed and scored live | Candidate: a third work with a strong rhythm from Asia, described and linked | Hazards and controls: balances low to the floor, by agreement, with a solo version always offered; no lifts; partner contact by agreement and never required; tap is watched, not done in hard shoes on the class floor (taps damage floors and ankles without training); the warm-up before every dancing day. Food: the plan is for an invented dancer; no task asks about a student's own diet, weight or body (APPROACH "Sensitive ground"). Tap's history: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own page and a dance history archive's page on tap's African American and Irish roots first; Mantsoe: the artist's own published words first. Health's steps if a student tells something. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
326: | 3 | Level 1 | Making a dance. *How does a source become a dance, and how does a dance's shape serve what it is for?* | 34 | da-g9-ideas; da-g9-form | DA:Cr1.1.Ia; DA:Cr1.1.Ib; DA:Cr2.1.Ia; DA:Cr2.1.Ib | Creating (Cr1, Cr2); responding through the two works (review). Works: William Forsythe, One Flat Thing, reproduced (Germany, 2000; Forsythe born 1949, the unit plan records whether living, as of its date; the maker's or the company's page), described and linked: dancers among rows of tables, a structure of cues that serves the idea; Akram Khan and Sidi Larbi Cherkaoui, zero degrees (United Kingdom and Belgium, 2005; the company's page), described and linked: how a told story of a train journey at the India and Bangladesh border became a duet | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's inspiration list, movement challenge, start, middle and finish, and the four tools. DA:Cr1.1.Ia: try many sources of movement (a text, an image, a sound, an object, a place) for one piece and analyze in the process log how each shaped it. DA:Cr1.1.Ib: test dance elements to find the student's own movement strengths and choose demanding moves for an original piece. DA:Cr2.1.Ia: make a dance with others whose structure (theme and variation, ABA, accumulation, narrative) serves its purpose, and explain how. DA:Cr2.1.Ib: write an artist's statement for the dance and discuss how its structure and elements support it. Words: stimulus, strengths, structure, purpose, artist's statement, accumulation. Showing: the small-group dances, with their statements read aloud, for the class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cr1.1.Ia, a kept piece: the log entry for three sources tried and an analysis of how each shaped the piece; DA:Cr1.1.Ib, a kept piece with a look-for: the strengths test in the log and two demanding moves chosen, shown at the teacher's spot. End-of-unit assessment (a showing day, then the statement): DA:Cr2.1.Ia, a look-for on the showing: the structure named and visible, and a spoken line on how it serves the purpose; DA:Cr2.1.Ib, a kept piece: the artist's statement on structure and elements, drafted, given written feedback after the critique and revised. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: blending established movement and styles (Level 2). Tools: source cards, the strengths test sheet, structure cards, the statement frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: sources from the room, a library book or a public domain poem. Short path: 16 days | After unit 2 because a dance is made from clear elements and rehearsed phrases; before unit 4 because a dance must exist to be reworked and recorded. The artist-statement model starts here. The mid-year check follows it | Candidate: a choreographer from Africa or Latin America whose source is a place, described and linked | Hazards and controls: as unit 2; group making on a marked floor with zones so groups do not collide. zero degrees: the sensitivity agent reads the company's own published words first. One Flat Thing's tables are watched only: no student climbs on, jumps over or slides on a table. Sources from a student's own life are invited, never required, with a public or invented source marked the same. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
327: | 4 | Level 1 | Seeing patterns, changing a dance. *How do patterns carry a dance's purpose in its culture, and how does a choreographer know a change made it better?* | 38 | da-g9-revise; da-g9-respond | DA:Cr3.1.Ia; DA:Cr3.1.Ib; DA:Re7.1.Ia; DA:Re7.1.Ib; DA:Re8.1.Ia; DA:Re9.1.Ia | Creating (Cr3); responding (Re7, Re8, Re9). Works: Jiří Kylián, Sinfonietta (Nederlands Dans Theater, the Netherlands, 1978; the company's page), described and linked: repeated patterns that serve a purpose; Emily Johnson, Niicugni (United States, 2012; Johnson, a Yup'ik choreographer, Catalyst Dance; the unit plan records whether living, as of its date), described and linked to the artist's page; Gregory Maqoma, Exit/Exist (South Africa, 2012, a solo on the life of the Xhosa chief Maqoma; the artist's page), described and linked | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's try-a-change routine, motif symbols, describe and interpret, and the criteria card. DA:Re7.1.Ia: analyze how repeated movement patterns and the dancers' relationships serve a dance's purpose. DA:Re7.1.Ib: analyze how elements across at least two styles and traditions (the three works) carry intent in their cultural setting. DA:Re8.1.Ia: compare two dances, discussing intent and expression and how elements and setting strengthen the meaning. DA:Re9.1.Ia: analyze a dance's expression and discuss what was learned, using evaluation criteria and dance terms. DA:Cr3.1.Ia: rework the devices and structure of the unit 3 dance to make its purpose clearer and evaluate in the log what each revision did. DA:Cr3.1.Ib: compare two established notation methods (motif notation and Labanotation) by recording one 8-count section in each. Words: pattern, relationship, cultural context, criteria, revision, notation. Showing: the revised dance beside its first version, for the class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment (a written sitting on an unseen Curio Class dance and a written description of a second): DA:Re7.1.Ia, a written item: a repeated pattern and the purpose it serves; DA:Re7.1.Ib, a written item: how an element carries intent in each dance's stated cultural setting. End-of-unit assessment: DA:Re8.1.Ia, a kept piece: a comparison of two studied dances, drafted, given written feedback and revised; DA:Re9.1.Ia, a written item in the end sitting: the unseen dance's expression analyzed with three criteria and dance terms; DA:Cr3.1.Ia, a look-for on the showing and the log: two revisions to devices or structure and an evaluation of each; DA:Cr3.1.Ib, a kept piece: one section recorded in both methods with two differences named. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: weighing societal values (Level 2), a whole dance notated (Level 3). Tools: work cards, the notation sheets for both methods, the criteria card, the change log, Curio Class's own. Free path: pencil and printed sheets. Short path: 19 days | After unit 3 because the revision reworks unit 3's dance and the analysis uses its structure words; before unit 5 because the concert shows the revised dance and research asks for the analysis habits | Candidate: a dance from Asia for the comparison, described and linked | Niicugni: the sensitivity agent reads Emily Johnson's own published words first; the unit names her as a Yup'ik maker in her own words, the dance is watched only, and no student is asked about their own heritage. Maqoma's solo tells of a chief who fought colonial rule: the agent reads the artist's own published words first; the unit tells it plainly from them, with no role play. Hazards and controls: as unit 2. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
328: | 5 | Level 1 | The concert and the question. *How do research and design shape a dance for an audience, and why do dances from different times differ?* | 34 | da-g9-stage; da-g9-connect | DA:Pr6.1.Ia; DA:Pr6.1.Ib; DA:Cn10.1.Ia; DA:Cn10.1.Ib; DA:Cn11.1.Ia | Performing (Pr6); connecting (Cn10, Cn11); creating as the team makes its answer dance. Works: Asadata Dafora (born in Sierra Leone, 1890 to 1965), Kykunkor (New York, 1934), a dance drama he staged from West African dance and music, described and linked to a dance history archive's page; August Bournonville, La Sylphide (Denmark, 1836, a Romantic ballet still danced by the Royal Danish Ballet), described and linked to the company's page | Retrieval in day 2's warm-up: the bridge's stage terms and etiquette, the research notes and the like-me line. DA:Cn10.1.Ia: analyze with evidence a dance's ideas and how the choreographer's view (Dafora showing West African dance to a New York audience) shapes the student's reading. DA:Cn10.1.Ib: research a dance question with a team (for example, how did a social dance travel between two places) and make a dance that answers it, then compare the team's process with a classmate team's. DA:Cn11.1.Ia: compare dances from two chosen styles or periods (La Sylphide and Kykunkor, or one of them beside a unit 3 work) and explain why they differ through their people's views. DA:Pr6.1.Ia: show leadership and etiquette in rehearsal, apply notes, and evaluate documented rehearsal methods with proper terms. DA:Pr6.1.Ib: evaluate production design options (light, space, costume as plain color, sound) and carry out the ones that best serve the dance's purpose. Words: research question, evidence, period, production design, etiquette, notes. Showing: the Level 1 concert for families (unit 4's revised dance and the team answer dances), with the family's yes, or the student's own at 18 or over, for any showing outside class. Evidence, id by id. Mid-unit assessment: DA:Cn10.1.Ia, a written item in a sitting: the ideas of a described dance and how its maker's view shapes the reading, with evidence; DA:Cn11.1.Ia, a kept piece: the two-period comparison with reasons, drafted, given written feedback and revised. End-of-unit assessment (the concert, then the process record): DA:Cn10.1.Ib, a look-for on the answer dance and a kept piece: the team's question, its research notes and the process comparison; DA:Pr6.1.Ia, a look-for in the dress rehearsal and a kept piece: notes applied and an evaluation of two documented rehearsal methods in proper terms; DA:Pr6.1.Ib, a kept piece with a look-for: two design options judged and the chosen one carried out in the concert. Each item is marked met, partly or not yet. Out: a whole concert planned by the class (Level 2). Tools: research note cards, the production design sheet, the rehearsal evaluation sheet, a concert program frame, Curio Class's own. Free path: house lights and a doorway, plain clothes in one color. Short path: 17 days | Last in Level 1 because the concert shows the revised dance and the year's making, and research needs the analysis habits of unit 4; the production-plan model starts here. Its concert in weeks 31 and 32 is its own end-of-unit assessment, before the year-end sitting | Candidate: a third period's dance from Asia for the comparison, described and linked | Kykunkor stages a ritual story: the unit studies it by reading and watching only; no student dances or imitates it (STANDARD.md 5 leave-out line); the sensitivity agent reads a dance history archive's page and Dafora's own published words first. La Sylphide's Romantic ideal of the dancer is watched, never tied to a student's body. A research question from a student's own family is optional, with a public question marked the same. Production hazards and controls: no student on a ladder, cables taped, no haze, no strobe. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
329: | 6 | Level 2 | Back on the floor (review and placement). *What does a Level 1 dancer bring, and where does each of us start?* | 6 |  | None | Performing, creating and responding as review of Level 1. Works: the class's own Level 1 portfolio pieces; one Level 1 work revisited (Sinfonietta, unit 4) | The Level 2 review unit (STANDARD.md 3.2): at most 6 days, counted inside the standards, that doubles as placement. Review: the warm-up, balances, syncopation, the artist's statement, motif notation, the criteria card and the research notes, each at Level 1 depth. Placement: a student new to the school shows a phrase, a short statement and a written analysis scored against the Level 1 year-end check and unit 5's guide. Words: review, placement, goal. Showing: each student's short Level 1 phrase, re-danced, for the class. Evidence: the readiness record sorts each student met, partly or not yet on each thread; placement marks go to the counselor's note. No Level 2 id is scored here. Out: every Level 2 id (units 7 to 10). Tools: the review cards, the placement sheet, Curio Class's own. Short path: 3 days | First in Level 2 because a student may arrive from Level 1 the year before, after a gap, or new to the school; the review is the readiness check for every Level 2 row and its results shape units 7 and 8 | Candidate: a self-check form a student new to the school can fill in before day 1 | Hazards and controls: as unit 1; a student new to dance placed by mistake moves to Level 1 without a mark against them. Agent reads: sensitivity; safety (moving unit). |
